diff --git a/docs/docker-deployment.md b/docs/docker-deployment.md
index f6eca78..e048a5b 100644
--- a/docs/docker-deployment.md
+++ b/docs/docker-deployment.md
@@ -124,6 +124,23 @@ If you're using a provider other than AWS, you will also need some of the follow
 If your storage provider is on a different site than your Fizzy instance and doesn't return CORS headers on presigned URL responses, inline images may fail to load.
 In that case, set `SERVICE_WORKER_CORS_ENABLED=false` so the service worker fetches uploaded files without CORS mode.
 
+#### Push notifications (optional)
+
+Fizzy can send push notifications to people's phones and desktops, so whatever lands in their notifications tray reaches them there too.
+Each person turns them on for the device they're using from their notification settings.
+
+To enable them, Fizzy needs a VAPID key pair, which identifies your instance to the browsers' push services.
+Generate one by running:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails web_push:generate_vapid_keys
+```
+
+Then set the two values it prints in the `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` and `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` environment variables.
+Keep the same key pair from then on: if it changes, people have to turn push notifications on again on each device.
+
+Push notifications need your instance to be served over HTTPS, and your server must be able to reach the push services over HTTPS (`fcm.googleapis.com`, `updates.push.services.mozilla.com`, `web.push.apple.com`, and `*.notify.windows.com`).
+
 #### Multi-tenant mode
 
 By default, when you run the Fizzy Docker image you'll be limited to creating a single account (although that account can have as many users as you like).
